\section{Differences between \code{Brazil/B3} and \code{Brazil/BMF}}{

The two calendars have the same weekdays and settings, and every holiday of
\code{Brazil/BMF} is also a holiday of \code{Brazil/B3}. They are equal from 1990 to
2000. Since 2001 \code{Brazil/B3} has these extra days without a trading session:
\itemize{
\item December 24th and the last weekday of the year (December 31st, or the
Friday before it when December 31st falls on a weekend). Banks are open
for internal work on these days, so they are business days in
\code{Brazil/BMF} and \code{Brazil/ANBIMA}.
\item The São Paulo holidays January 25th and July 9th, from 2001 to 2021
(except 2020-07-09, when B3 was open). Since 2022 B3 trades on these days.
\item November 20th (Black Consciousness Day) from 2006 to 2019, when it was a
São Paulo city holiday only (it was not a closing day in 2020). Since 2024 it is a national holiday and it
is in both calendars.
\item 2014-06-12, the opening day of the World Cup in São Paulo.
}

Up to 2027 the \code{Brazil/B3} holidays come from the calendars published by
B3. After 2027 they are estimated by a rule: the \code{Brazil/ANBIMA} holidays
plus December 24th and the last weekday of the year.
